#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Bring BackOtter test guests up to date, on every test host at once.

Usage: vm-update [--try | --probe] [--hosts H,...] [--jobs N] [--cpus N] [--memory MB]
                 [--out DIR] [--hook before=FILE]... [--hook after=FILE]... [--push LOCAL=REMOTE]...
                 [--write-fingerprint] [--merge-gate CMD] [--keep-failed] GUEST...
       vm-update --status|--finish [--hosts H,...] GUEST...
       vm-update --reproject [--hosts H,...] GUEST...
       vm-update --hold-fleet-lock RUN

Run on the host that has the guests (Chopin), from anywhere: GUEST is a
folder in vms/ next to this script's testvm/ (or under BKO_WORK).  For each
GUEST, vm-update

  1. checks that nothing uses the guest's images, here or on the other hosts
     (a QEMU program with them open, or a qcow2 overlay on top of them), and
     that each host's images are still the ones the last update (or the
     copy) left there: the SHA-256s recorded in vms/GUEST/image-state, and
     the same generation as here;
  2. boots the guest with vm-run --ram --keep (its vm-run-options, the
     passphrase typed when it has one), so that every write goes to an
     overlay, beside the image or in RAM (VM_UPDATE_RAM), and the image
     itself is only read, once this host has memory to spare for the VM;
  3. lists its packages (update/pkgset.sh), then runs update/in-guest.sh in
     it over SSH: the guest's own package manager (apt, dnf, pacman, zypper,
     xbps, apk, moss, emerge on Gentoo, building from source only what an
     allowlist names, nixos-rebuild, pkg, syspatch and pkg_add, pkg on
     OmniOS, pkgman on Haiku, hbsd-update on HardenedBSD), unattended, after
     pointing a guest that its builder froze at its distribution's live
     repositories; as root, or as the user named in vms/GUEST/ssh-user
     (Haiku's "user", which is uid 0: Haiku has no root);
  4. reboots it and waits for SSH again, so a broken kernel or boot loader
     stops here, and lists the packages again.  The merge rule: there is an
     update to merge when the two listings differ, or in-guest.sh printed a
     "step:" line (a fix no package shows), or either listing is unknown.
     With nothing to merge, the guest is powered off and its overlay thrown
     away: no trim, no new generation.  With --merge-gate, its command then
     decides: exit 0 merges, exit 10 doesn't, and the overlay goes.  Next a
     `bko-holt dump` for the fingerprint (update/fingerprint.py), and
     update/trim.sh in it (fstrim, zpool trim: vm-run --discard makes the room
     the guest frees, its old packages and downloads, zero clusters that hold
     no data in the overlay; the merge carries them into the image, where
     retaking the snapshot frees them), then it is powered off;
  5. copies the overlay (only the blocks the update wrote) to every other
     host, checks step 1 again on every host, and then merges the overlay
     into the image (qemu-img commit) on one host at a time, this one last,
     so that a host always holds a good image; retakes the "fresh-install"
     snapshot, gives the clusters that freed back to the host's file system
     (qcow2-punch.py) and records the new SHA-256s and generation in
     image-state, with image-fingerprint.json and image-stat.json beside it.

So the hosts' images stay identical in what the guest sees, while only the
update crosses the network.  (Not byte-identical: the snapshot tables carry
each host's own timestamps, hence image-state.)  A guest that nothing can
update (NetBSD: a release's base sets) is reported and left alone.

  --try         stop after step 4 and throw the overlay away: test that the
                guest updates and still boots, changing nothing.  No trim, no
                dump; result.json says what would merge
  --probe       boot the guest and run its hooks only: no update, no reboot.
                With --write-fingerprint also its listing and dump, which
                make its image-fingerprint.json on every host (the hosts must
                be at one generation)
  --hosts HOST,...
                the other hosts (default: chopin and cinqcent, but this
                one); --hosts '' updates only this host's copy, after
                which the others no longer match it
  --jobs N      update N guests at once (default 3); each takes a 2 GB VM,
                its overlay and a free SSH port, and waits for the memory it
                needs
  --cpus N      the VM's CPUs instead of vm-run's 2 (DragonFly rebuilds its
                base system from source: about 25 min with 8 and --memory
                6144, the whole update)
  --memory MB   the VM's RAM instead of vm-run's 2048
  --out DIR     update.log, serial.log, result.json, the two listings, the
                steps and the hooks' outputs go in DIR (DIR/GUEST with several
                guests); the guest's own
                update.log gets one line saying so.  Without it, result.json
                goes in vms/GUEST/.vm-update-last/
  --hook before=FILE, --hook after=FILE
                run FILE in the guest with sh, after SSH is up and before the
                update, or after the reboot; its output goes in
                DIR/hook-NAME.out, NAME the file's name less any extension
                (a second of one name: NAME-2).  A hook ends by
                printing "=== end".  One that fails, or doesn't get there,
                doesn't stop the run, and no merge follows it
  --push LOCAL=REMOTE
                copy LOCAL to the guest's REMOTE, executable, before the hooks
  --write-fingerprint
                with --probe, as above.  A real update always writes one
  --merge-gate CMD
                a real update's last word, run with sh on this host after the
                after-hooks and before the trim: exit 0 merges, exit 10 is a
                blocked merge, anything else a failure that doesn't merge
  --keep-failed with --try or --probe: a run that fails leaves its folder
                (its overlays and logs) for the caller, named in result.json
  --status      only step 1's checks: whether the hosts agree, and what uses
                the images (and, with those checked, image-stat.json if the
                host has none)
  --finish      complete merges that step 5 left undone on some host (it
                says so, and keeps the overlay there for this)
  --reproject   recompute the guests' fingerprints from their stored dumps
                (HOST_ROOT/guest-dumps/GUEST/GEN.*), with no boot, after
                fingerprint.py or holttest1d/judge.py changed.  A digest that
                moved goes in the sidecar's `was`
  --hold-fleet-lock RUN
                for GuestTest2 update's driver: take the fleet lock for round
                RUN, print "held", and keep it while a line arrives on stdin
                at least every 120 s

A real update or --finish holds the fleet lock, vms/.vm-update.fleet.lock on
the first host, exclusively for as long as it runs: one driver at a time,
from either host.  A job of a GuestTest2 update round (VM_UPDATE_ROUND=RUN)
holds it shared instead, and needs .vm-update.fleet.round to name RUN.
--hold-fleet-lock removes that file when it lets go, whatever the cause
(end of input, no heartbeat, TERM or HUP), so no later job of a round whose
holder is gone passes the check.

Environment:
  VM_UPDATE_RAM=GIB        the overlays in /mnt/bko-ram, kept there up to the
                           merge, under the reservation of GIB this job holds
                           from `cmp-run.sh --reserve` (an ancestor's row, or
                           it fails, and never falls back to disk).  One guest
                           to a reservation.  A run that outgrows it, or
                           outlives it, is stopped.  Unset: beside the image
  VM_UPDATE_ROUND=RUN      this job belongs to round RUN
  BKO_WORK                 the tree holding vms/ instead of this script's
  VM_UPDATE_REMOTE_VMS     the other hosts' vms/, relative to their home.  A
                           tree of its own (BKO_WORK) must name it before
                           anything looks at or changes the other hosts: the
                           default is the real fleet's
  VM_UPDATE_HOLT_BIN       the bko-holt set for the dump (default
                           /bkoscratch/holttest1d/bin)
  VM_UPDATE_LIMIT_KIB, VM_UPDATE_FREE_KIB
                           tests only: the overrun limit and the RAM disk's
                           free room, in place of the real ones

Real updates run only from a synced tree (this script at WORK/testvm, vms/
not a link), and neither they, --finish, --reproject nor --probe
--write-fingerprint run on the real tree with T2_HOST_ROOT or T2_WORK pointing
elsewhere: a test's world has a tree of its own.

Logs: vms/GUEST/update.log (what ran, with the guest's output) and
vms/GUEST/update-serial.log (the serial console), or --out's.  Stopping
vm-update (Ctrl-C, or a hangup or TERM) stops its VMs and deletes their
overlays, changing nothing; merges already under way finish first.
"""

